premises.content:
The effective low-energy tight-binding Hamiltonian for the emergent honeycomb Wannier lattice formed by the extrema of the pseudo-magnetic field in a buckled graphene superlattice is
      $$
      \mathcal{H}=-\tilde{\mu}\sum_{i}\sum_{s,\tau}c_{is\tau}^{\dagger}c_{is\tau}
      +m\sum_{i}\sum_{s,\tau}(\sigma_{z})_{ii}c_{is\tau}^{\dagger}c_{is\tau}
      -i\tilde{t}\sum_{s,\tau}\sum_{\langle i,j\rangle}c_{is\tau}^{\dagger}c_{js\tau}
      +i\lambda\sum_{s,\tau}\sum_{\langle\langle i,j\rangle\rangle}(\tau_{z})_{\kappa\kappa}\eta_{ij}c_{is\tau}^{\dagger}c_{js\tau},
      $$
      where $c_{is\tau}^{\dagger}$ ($c_{is\tau}$) creates (annihilates) an electron at effective Wannier site $i$ with spin $s$ and valley index $\tau$ (the emergent valley associated with the mini-Brillouin zone of the superlattice), $\sigma_{i}$ and $\tau_{i}$ are Pauli matrices acting on sublattice and valley (mini-valley) degrees of freedom, $\eta_{ij}=\pm1$ denotes clockwise/anticlockwise second-neighbor hopping signs, $\tilde{\mu}$ is an overall onsite energy, $m$ is a sublattice imbalance (staggered onsite potential between the two Wannier sublattices), $\tilde{t}$ is the nearest-neighbor hopping on the emergent honeycomb, and $\lambda$ is a valley-dependent second-neighbor hopping amplitude. This Hamiltonian is equivalent in symmetry structure to the Kane–Mele model under the identification of the valley isospin ($\tau$) with the spin in the Kane–Mele model and the mini-valley indices $\kappa$ and $\kappa'$ with the valleys $K$ and $K'$ in the Kane–Mele model. It reproduces the band dispersion of the active bands (within the energy window $[-0.025t, 0.025t]$ of the full-lattice band structure, where $t$ is the graphene nearest-neighbor hopping) of the buckled graphene superlattice near the Fermi level.

